WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.660
Let the n coefficients of h be
positive or negative one, and

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:07.880
form the circulant matrix from that row
and its successive right cyclic shifts.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:11.160
Define h star by replacing
X with X inverse.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:17.160
We claim that H times H transpose
equals n times the identity if and

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:22.980
only if h times h star equals n, in the
integer group ring of the cyclic group.

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:27.180
First, suppose the matrix is Hadamard.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:31.740
The first row is orthogonal to
every nontrivial cyclic shift.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:35.680
By the correlation identity,
every nonconstant coefficient

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:39.330
of h times h star is therefore zero.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:43.480
The constant coefficient is the sum
of the squares of the row entries.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:47.660
Every coefficient is a
sign, so this sum is n.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:51.600
The product is exactly
the constant polynomial n.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:56.800
Conversely, start with n sign
coefficients and assume their

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:01.060
polynomial satisfies h
times h star equals n.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:04.460
Form the circulant matrix
from those coefficients.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:09.680
Uniqueness of the representative
of degree less than n says that

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:13.230
each nonconstant coefficient
of the product is zero.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:17.140
Thus the first row is orthogonal
to every nontrivial cyclic shift.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:22.960
Why does that give every pair
of rows? Number rows from zero.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:28.620
Compare row r and row s, then
shift both back by r places.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.700
A common shift does not
change a dot product.

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:36.320
We are left with row zero
and the shift by s minus r.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:41.560
In formulas, substitute
k equal to j minus r.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:47.640
The resulting sum is the coefficient of
X to the s minus r in h times h star.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:52.220
It is zero for distinct
rows, and n for equal rows.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:57.140
Hence H times H transpose
is n times the identity.

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:01.700
The sign assumption is part
of the dictionary. Forward,

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:04.780
it makes the squared norm exactly n.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:08.440
Backward, it guarantees
that every entry of

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:11.390
the constructed matrix is
positive or negative one.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:15.600
The polynomial equation alone
does not guarantee those entries.

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:22.000
For example, in dimension four
take the constant polynomial two.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.240
Its product with its star is four.

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:30.496
But its coefficient list
is two, zero, zero, zero.

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:35.240
The circulant matrix is twice
the identity: orthogonal rows,

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.800
yet its entries are not
signs. It is not Hadamard.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:45.180
For another useful check, take the
all ones row in dimension four.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:50.590
Every shift is the same row, so two
distinct rows have dot product four.

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:54.270
The polynomial product has
every coefficient equal to four,

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.640
rather than only its constant coefficient.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:00.820
Circulant symmetry alone
does not give orthogonality.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:05.540
With the sign hypothesis included,
both implications are proved.

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:09.360
Many matrix conditions have
become one polynomial equation.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:14.180
In the next main node, we will ask what
that equation tells us about the order n.
